    id: 'sov12-the-engineering-disaster',
    title: 'The Engineering Disaster - bigger is not better, and wisdom is profitable to direct',
    bigIdea: 'The Kingdom never measured strength by size — "Not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit, saith the LORD of hosts" (Zechariah 4:6). The industry bet the opposite: pour more compute, data, and money into A.I. and it will always get better ("scaling laws"). An Atlantic essay now calls generative A.I. an "engineering disaster": the brute-force bet is hitting diminishing returns and draining water and power while smaller models catch up. The Word saw the pattern — blunt iron met with MORE force is folly; "wisdom is profitable to direct" (Ecclesiastes 10:10). That verse is this whole class: sovereign, small, well-engineered over big and wasteful.',
    inApp: 'NOTICE where you are tempted to answer a problem with MORE — more compute, more spending, more hours, more storage — instead of a sharper edge. TEST it against Ecclesiastes 10:10: is this blunt-iron-plus-strength, or have you whetted the edge (found the wiser, leaner way)? CAPTURE one place you are pouring effort into a "bag with holes" (Haggai 1:6) — a scaling that isn\'t yielding. REDIRECT: whet one edge this week — the efficient fix, the right-sized tool — and where you\'re in a sunk-cost hole, stop digging and turn (Proverbs 28:13).',
