    lesson: 'FIRST, THE KINGDOM NEVER MEASURED POWER BY SIZE. This week\'s source is a video Darrell forwarded (Ayana of the Center for Democratic AI, quoting an Atlantic essay, "Generative AI is an Engineering Disaster"): by economic and engineering measures, she argues, generative A.I. may be one of the worst technologies ever deployed. Before we weigh that, set the Kingdom\'s plumb line — it has always refused the world\'s math of bigger-equals-stronger: "Not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit, saith the LORD of hosts." (Zechariah 4:6). "Some trust in chariots, and some in horses: but we will remember the name of the LORD our God." (Psalms 20:7). "The horse is prepared against the day of battle: but safety is of the LORD." (Proverbs 21:31). You may prepare the biggest horse; the outcome was never in the horse. Hold that; the whole lesson is that verse meeting a trillion-dollar bet on horses. SECOND, THE BET — SCALING LAWS. Early A.I. leaned on "scaling laws": rough predictions of how well a model performs from four parameters — model size, dataset size, cost, and error rate. The belief was simple and seductive: pump in more power, more compute, more data, and performance keeps climbing. It worked for a while, so the biggest companies made a massive bet on it — and, the speaker argues, they are now stuck with a theory that has stopped paying. (Carried as the video and the Atlantic essay report it; the figures below are theirs, not independently re-verified here — DR-0076 SS8.) THIRD, DIMINISHING RETURNS — THE BAG WITH HOLES. The brute-force method is plateauing: smaller models now perform about as well as massive resource-heavy ones; companies are running out of fresh, human-generated data to train on; and adding more parameters and compute yields only marginal gains, not the old exponential leaps. The Word named this economy of effort-without-yield precisely: "Ye have sown much, and bring in little... and he that earneth wages earneth wages to put it into a bag with holes." (Haggai 1:6). And it named the reason such wealth evaporates: "Wealth gotten by vanity shall be diminished: but he that gathereth by labour shall increase." (Proverbs 13:11). More poured into a torn bag is not progress; it is the diminishing the proverb promised. FOURTH, THE RESOURCE DRAIN — AND THE BROKEN CISTERNS. Despite the plateau, the industry keeps pushing "more is better," and the bill is physical: rising electricity and water use, heavier demand for hardware and chip metals, and (the speaker reports) plans for some 1,500 new U.S. data centers to feed the inefficient scaling. This is a stewardship question before it is an engineering one. Man was placed in the garden "to dress it and to keep it" (Genesis 2:15) — to tend, not to strip. And Jeremiah\'s image is almost literal here: "they have forsaken me the fountain of living waters, and hewed them out cisterns, broken cisterns, that can hold no water." (Jeremiah 2:13). Vast systems that consume the water and hold nothing lasting are exactly the broken cistern — labor and rivers poured into a vessel that cannot keep what it takes. FIFTH, BIGGER IS NOT BETTER — BABEL AND THE BARNS. The deeper error is spiritual: treating SCALE itself as the goal, a name to be made. The first such project is on record: "let us build us a city and a tower, whose top may reach unto heaven; and let us make us a name" (Genesis 11:4) — height and size as identity, which Yahweh scattered. Jesus told it at the level of a single life: the man whose answer to abundance was only MORE storage — "I will pull down my barns, and build greater" (Luke 12:18) — heard the verdict "Thou fool, this night thy soul shall be required of thee" (Luke 12:20), "So is he that layeth up treasure for himself, and is not rich toward God." (Luke 12:21). Building greater barns for their own sake is the parable of the moment: more capacity mistaken for more worth. SIXTH, WISDOM OVER BRUTE FORCE — THE ANCHOR. The speaker\'s solution is not anti-A.I.; it is better ENGINEERING — it is entirely possible to build good models on less money, data, water, and compute, but efficient code takes real, intensive work, while blundering code is easy. That is Ecclesiastes exactly: "If the iron be blunt, and he do not whet the edge, then must he put to more strength: but wisdom is profitable to direct." (Ecclesiastes 10:10). A blunt axe swung harder is the whole scaling strategy; whetting the edge — wisdom, craft, efficiency — is the alternative. "Wisdom is better than strength" (Ecclesiastes 9:16); "Wisdom is better than weapons of war: but one sinner destroyeth much good." (Ecclesiastes 9:18). The Kingdom always prized the sharpened edge over the bigger swing. SEVENTH, THE SUNK-COST TRAP — AND THE WAY OUT IS TO TURN. Why won\'t the giants pivot to efficiency? The speaker (citing an OpenAI co-founder) says re-engineering a product already carrying trillion-dollar valuations is brutally hard: costly, foundational, and frightening to investors — so they keep pushing inefficient A.I. because they have "dug a hole too deep." That is the sunk-cost fallacy given a suit, and the Word calls it by its older names: "Pride goeth before destruction, and an haughty spirit before a fall." (Proverbs 16:18); "As a dog returneth to his vomit, so a fool returneth to his folly." (Proverbs 26:11). Jesus prescribed the prevention — count first: "For which of you, intending to build a tower, sitteth not down first, and counteth the cost, whether he have sufficient to finish it?" (Luke 14:28). And the cure once you are in the hole is never to keep digging but to turn: "He that covereth his sins shall not prosper: but whoso confesseth and forsaketh them shall have mercy." (Proverbs 28:13). Confess-and-forsake is a repentance principle, and it is also sound engineering: stop defending the buried cost; change course. EIGHTH, OUR LANE — SOVEREIGN, SMALL, AND SHARP. This is not a lesson we watch from the outside; it is the thesis this whole class was built on. From Week 1\'s picture — the generator in the garage, a right-sized tool you own and can run — to every week since, the house has argued for SOVEREIGN and SMALL over rented and gigantic: models sized to the real need, run on iron we steward, code whetted for efficiency instead of fed by brute scale, water and power treated as a trust. The industry\'s "engineering disaster," if the essay is right, is what happens when a culture worships the horse and forgets the LORD of the outcome; our answer is Ecclesiastes 10:10 built into a stack — wisdom profitable to direct. And we hold the claim honestly (DR-0076 SS8): the Atlantic\'s and the speaker\'s figures — smaller models matching larger, the data wall, the 1,500 data centers — are carried as reported, not independently re-verified here; where a number is load-bearing we would fetch it before betting on it. The point that does not depend on any single figure is the plumb line we started with: "Not by might, nor by power, but by my spirit, saith the LORD of hosts." (Zechariah 4:6).',
