    lesson: 'FIRST, SETTLE THE REGISTER: WE DO NOT OPERATE ON FEAR. Before a single headline, fix the posture the Word commands: "For God hath not given us the spirit of fear; but of power, and of love, and of a sound mind." (2 Timothy 1:7). Every "disruption," "apocalypse," and "the end of X" story is first a test of WHICH SPIRIT is reading it — fear, or a sound mind. SECOND, THIS WEEK\'S SOURCE, held to that plumb line. A tech piece Darrell forwarded (The Daily Upside) reports that Salesforce beat its fiscal second-quarter expectations, its stock jumped more than 20%, and it raised guidance — and that CEO Marc Benioff called the "SaaSpocalypse," the loud thesis that A.I. agents would consume the software-as-a-service industry, "nonsense": "We have been hearing about this... these dire predictions about the end of software and how the models eat everything. But none of them have come true for us." The report adds an expanded Anthropic partnership ("Claudeforce"). Provenance honesty (DR-0076): the earnings, the stock move, and the quoted CEO are public and established fact; WHO ultimately wins the A.I.-versus-SaaS question is forecast and opinion — and the article itself is honest that analysts still see "material risk to many names." THIRD, THE WORD\'S TEST FOR A DOOM-PROPHECY IS FULFILLMENT. Scripture hands us the exact instrument to weigh a prediction of doom: "When a prophet speaketh in the name of the LORD, if the thing follow not, nor come to pass, that is the thing which the LORD hath not spoken, but the prophet hath spoken it presumptuously: thou shalt not be afraid of him." (Deuteronomy 18:22). The doomsayers said software was ending; by the CEO\'s account "none of them have come true." A word that does not come to pass was spoken presumptuously — and the command about such a word is precise: do not be afraid of it. FOURTH, DO NOT BE TOSSED — NOT BY THE PANIC, NOT BY THE RELIEF. The same market that erased close to a trillion dollars in software value on the FEAR then rallied on the RELIEF — the very picture the Word warns against: "That we henceforth be no more children, tossed to and fro, and carried about with every wind of doctrine." (Ephesians 4:14); "he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed." (James 1:6); "A double minded man is unstable in all his ways." (James 1:8). The discipline that replaces the swing is one verse: "Prove all things; hold fast that which is good." (1 Thessalonians 5:21). FIFTH, THE SERVANT DOES NOT EAT THE MASTER — TOOLS WERE MADE FOR MAN. The fear was stated plainly — "the models eat everything." The reported reality is the reverse of annihilation: the frontier A.I. companies are themselves Salesforce customers — the models CONSUMING and integrating the software, the tool serving rather than devouring. This is the created order the Word already fixed: "The sabbath was made for man, and not man for the sabbath" (Mark 2:27) — the ordinance serves the person, never the reverse; and "Thou madest him to have dominion over the works of thy hands; thou hast put all things under his feet" (Psalms 8:6). What was made to serve you is not to be feared as though it were your master. SIXTH, BUT REFUSE THE EUPHORIA TOO (THE HONEST SECOND TIER). The plumb line cuts both directions (DR-0100): a 20% relief rally is not vindication, and a raised forecast is not prophecy. Some names genuinely do face disruption — the article says so. So we neither gaslight the doom nor swallow the hype: "Let your moderation be known unto all men." (Philippians 4:5); "to think soberly, according as God hath dealt to every man the measure of faith." (Romans 12:3); "Be sober, be vigilant." (1 Peter 5:8). SEVENTH, DO NOT BOAST OF TOMORROW. A blowout quarter and a raised guidance are still claims on a tomorrow no company controls: "Boast not thyself of to morrow; for thou knowest not what a day may bring forth." (Proverbs 27:1). James names the whole habit and its cure: "Go to now, ye that say, To day or to morrow we will go into such a city... and buy and sell, and get gain: Whereas ye know not what shall be on the morrow. For what is your life? It is even a vapour... For that ye ought to say, If the Lord will, we shall live, and do this, or that." (James 4:13-15). EIGHTH, THE FEAR ITSELF WAS THE REAL SNARE. Notice what actually did the damage: not an agent that replaced the software, but the PANIC — nearly a trillion dollars evaporated on a prediction that had not happened. "The fear of man bringeth a snare: but whoso putteth his trust in the LORD shall be safe." (Proverbs 29:25). The antidote is not a better forecast; it is a different trust: "Fear thou not; for I am with thee: be not dismayed; for I am thy God." (Isaiah 41:10); "There is no fear in love; but perfect love casteth out fear." (1 John 4:18). NINTH, BUILD ON THE ROCK, NOT ON THE RALLY. Forecasts reverse and partnerships get announced and unwound; the house that survives every hype cycle is the Word actually done: "Therefore whosoever heareth these sayings of mine, and doeth them, I will liken him unto a wise man, which built his house upon a rock." (Matthew 7:24); and over all the churning devices of the market, "There are many devices in a man’s heart; nevertheless the counsel of the LORD, that shall stand." (Proverbs 19:21). TENTH, OUR LANE — SOVEREIGN, SOBER, AND UNAFRAID. This is the class thesis meeting a fear-cycle headline. Weigh every doom (and every shiny) prediction by whether it comes to pass (Deuteronomy 18:22). Keep tools in their place as servants (Mark 2:27), keep an exit from every vendor, and hold the whole scene under the true Owner — "The earth is the LORD’s, and the fulness thereof" (Psalms 24:1). We carry the headline honestly (DR-0076): the earnings and the 20% move are established; the winner of the A.I.-versus-SaaS question is not — and the load-bearing truth needs no forecast at all: "God hath not given us the spirit of fear... but a sound mind" (2 Timothy 1:7), and "the counsel of the LORD, that shall stand" (Proverbs 19:21).',
