    lesson: 'FIRST, SETTLE WHOSE COUNSEL SETS THE PATH. Before any headline: "Blessed is the man that walketh not in the counsel of the ungodly, nor standeth in the way of sinners, nor sitteth in the seat of the scornful." (Psalms 1:1); "Trust in the LORD with all thine heart; and lean not unto thine own understanding. In all thy ways acknowledge him, and he shall direct thy paths." (Proverbs 3:5-6). Whom you trust to guide a decision - a purchase or a life - is the whole question. SECOND, THIS WEEK\'S SOURCE, held to that plumb line. A McKinsey piece Darrell forwarded reports that consumers, led by Gen Z, do NOT trust algorithm-driven advice - generative A.I. lands below 40% net trust - and even discount friends and family, yet they increasingly route their path-to-purchase THROUGH A.I. and social feeds anyway; it also names four trends: the technology-driven path to purchase, a broad health revolution, the experience economy, and the resourceful consumer. Provenance (DR-0076): the survey findings are carried as reported; how far the behavior spreads is forecast. THIRD, THE FOLLY NAMED - SEEKING GUIDANCE FROM WHAT YOU DISTRUST. To consult what you do not trust is exactly the double mind Scripture warns against: "he that wavereth is like a wave of the sea driven with the wind and tossed." (James 1:6); "A double minded man is unstable in all his ways." (James 1:8); and self-guided, "There is a way which seemeth right unto a man, but the end thereof are the ways of death." (Proverbs 14:12). FOURTH, THE ONE COUNSEL FULLY WORTH TRUSTING. The ground of trust is a Person who cannot lie: "It is better to trust in the LORD than to put confidence in man." (Psalms 118:8); "Cursed be the man that trusteth in man, and maketh flesh his arm" against "Blessed is the man that trusteth in the LORD, and whose hope the LORD is." (Jeremiah 17:5,7); "God is not a man, that he should lie... hath he said, and shall he not do it?" (Numbers 23:19); and the Guide He gives, "when he, the Spirit of truth, is come, he will guide you into all truth" (John 16:13). FIFTH, SO TEST EVERYTHING ELSE - TOOLS INCLUDED. A.I. and feeds are permitted instruments, never blind masters: "Prove all things; hold fast that which is good." (1 Thessalonians 5:21); the Bereans "searched the scriptures daily, whether those things were so" (Acts 17:11); "believe not every spirit, but try the spirits whether they are of God" (1 John 4:1); and the standard that judges every claim, "To the law and to the testimony: if they speak not according to this word, it is because there is no light in them." (Isaiah 8:20). SIXTH, THE HEALTH REVOLUTION, HONESTLY - KNOWING IS NOT DOING. The report notes wellness data multiplying while most people still miss their goals - the doer-gap again, "be ye doers of the word, and not hearers only, deceiving your own selves" (James 1:22). Hold both tiers: the body is "the temple of the Holy Ghost which is in you... therefore glorify God in your body" (1 Corinthians 6:19-20), yet "bodily exercise profiteth little: but godliness is profitable unto all things" (1 Timothy 4:8), and true prosperity is soul-anchored, "I wish above all things that thou mayest prosper and be in health, even as thy soul prospereth" (3 John 1:2). SEVENTH, THE EXPERIENCE ECONOMY, WEIGHED. Spending is shifting from goods to experiences - a real good and a real pointer - but Ecclesiastes already ran that experiment to its end: "Then I looked on all the works that my hands had wrought... and, behold, all was vanity and vexation of spirit, and there was no profit under the sun." (Ecclesiastes 2:11). So the Lord\'s instruction stands: "Lay not up for yourselves treasures upon earth... But lay up for yourselves treasures in heaven... For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also." (Matthew 6:19-21) - and the one experience that never fades is His presence, "Thou wilt shew me the path of life: in thy presence is fulness of joy; at thy right hand there are pleasures for evermore." (Psalms 16:11). EIGHTH, THE RESOURCEFUL CONSUMER, REDEEMED. Thrift, repair, durability, making the dollar go further - this is stewardship when it is done unto Him: "He that is faithful in that which is least is faithful also in much" (Luke 16:10); "Gather up the fragments that remain, that nothing be lost" (John 6:12); "Be thou diligent to know the state of thy flocks, and look well to thy herds" (Proverbs 27:23) - value built in, not promoted on later. NINTH, OUR LANE - TRUST HIS COUNSEL, TEST THE TOOLS, STEWARD WELL, LET HIM LEAD. The sovereign practice for the age of A.I. shopping is not to reject the tools nor to trust them blindly, but to seat trust where it belongs and prove the rest: acknowledge Him first (Proverbs 3:6), trust the LORD over man (Psalms 118:8), test every algorithm against His word (Isaiah 8:20; 1 Thessalonians 5:21), steward faithfully (Luke 16:10), and aim treasure and health at what lasts (Matthew 6:21; 3 John 1:2). TENTH, THE PROMISE THAT CLOSES IT. To the one who will trust and be led, He says: "I will instruct thee and teach thee in the way which thou shalt go: I will guide thee with mine eye." (Psalms 32:8). And this is the covering over everyone who follows His counsel rather than the crowd: "No weapon that is formed against thee shall prosper; and every tongue that shall rise against thee in judgment thou shalt condemn. This is the heritage of the servants of the LORD, and their righteousness is of me, saith the LORD." (Isaiah 54:17) - the voices of the age rise and pass, but the servant who follows the Word is vindicated by the LORD Himself. We carry the headline honestly (DR-0076): the distrust-yet-follow finding is reported, the spread is forecast - and the load-bearing truth needs no survey: "It is better to trust in the LORD than to put confidence in man" (Psalms 118:8).',
